Routing Redundancy Protocol
    - Network protocol that prevents disruptions in communication by automatically rerouting data traffic in case of a path or device failure

First Hop Redundancy Protocol (FHRP)
    - Set of protocols designed to ensure network reliability by provising automatic failover to a backup router to maintain uninterrupted network service
    (Like when there is construction on the road and there are signs and traffic controls that guide everyone to flow through the road more efficiently)

    Benefits: Reliability, Load Balancing, Seamless transitions

    To make this protocol work, we need two critical components:
        Virtual IP  -  IP address that is not bound to a specific device but serves as a representative for one or multiple devices
        Subinterface    -   Allows physical interface in a router or switch to be subdivided into a multiple logical interface; segments and manages networks


3 MAIN PROTOCOLS THAT ARE CONSIDERED TO BE FHRP
Hot Standby Router Protocol (HSRP) - Establishes a fault-tolerant default gateway for devices on a local network segment; Active Router and Standby Router (Takes over whenever active fail)

Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol (VRRP) - Similar to HSRP; Open Standard (Not tied to specific vendor and more compatible); Multiple router config

Gateway Load Balancing Protocol (GLBP) - Adding load balancing capabilities into the mix; Still allows multiple routers